  'c12-chem-006',
  12,
  'chemistry',
  'Haloalkanes and Haloarenes',
  'mcq',
  'medium',
  'Why is an SN1 nucleophilic substitution reaction of an optically active haloalkane (such as (R)-2-bromooctane) accompanied by partial racemisation rather than complete inversion?',
  '["Because the planar carbocation intermediate can be attacked by nucleophile from either face, but shielding by departing leaving group favours back-side attack","Because the reaction proceeds exclusively via a concerted pentacoordinate transition state","Because the front-side attack is energetically preferred over back-side attack","Because free radicals are formed which rotate rapidly in solution"]'::jsonb,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  NULL,
