  'Haloalkanes react with potassium cyanide (KCN) to yield primarily alkyl cyanides (R-CN), whereas with silver cyanide (AgCN) they form predominantly alkyl isocyanides (R-NC). What is the chemical reason for this divergence?',
  '["KCN is predominantly ionic, leaving the ambidentate cyanide ion free to attack through carbon (forming stronger C-C bond), while AgCN is predominantly covalent with Ag bonded to C, leaving nitrogen lone pair to attack","KCN reacts by an SN1 mechanism while AgCN reacts by an SN2 mechanism","Silver ion acts as a reducing agent while potassium ion acts as an oxidising agent","Alkyl isocyanides are more thermodynamically stable than alkyl cyanides"]'::jsonb,
